WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.240
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.179
You will not believe the things you will see
in this episode, I promise.

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:18.230
Earlier we saw that OpenAI’s GPT3 and Codex
AI techniques can be used to solve grade school

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:25.380
level math brain teasers, then, they were
improved to be able to solve university-level

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.970
math questions.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.580
Note that this technique required additional
help to do that.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:38.400
And, a followup work could even take a crack
at roughly a third of mathematical olympiad

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.090
level math problems.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:47.560
And now, let’s see what the excellent scientists
at DeepMind have been up to in the meantime.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.840
Check this out - this is AlphaCode.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.880
What is that?

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:59.130
Well, in college, computer scientists learn
how to teach the computers to program themselves.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:04.750
Now, DeepMind decided to instead, teach them
how to program themselves.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.750
Wow.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.300
Now, here you see an absolute miracle in the
making.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.360
Look.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.630
Here is the description of the problem, and
here is the solution.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.850
Well, I hear you saying, Károly, there is
no solution here.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.490
And you are indeed right, just give it a second.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.200
Yes, that’s right!

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.280
Now, hold on to your papers, and marvel at
how this AI is coding up the solution right

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.500
on front of our eyes!

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.620
But that’s nothing, we can also ask what
the neural network is looking at.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.900
Check this out!

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.280
It is peeping at different, important parts
of the problem statement, and proceeds to

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.490
write the solution taking these into consideration.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:57.630
You see that it also looks at different parts
of the previous code that it had written to

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.150
make sure that the new additions are consistent
with those.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.329
Wow.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:05.920
That is absolutely amazing.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.330
It almost feels like watching a human solve
this problem.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.260
Well, it solved this problem correctly.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.620
Unbelievable.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.569
So, how good is it?

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:24.200
Well, it can solve solve about 34% of the
problems in this dataset.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.090
What does that mean?

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.090
Is that good or bad?

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:33.180
Now, if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because it

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:39.400
means that it roughly matches the expertise
level of the average human competitor.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.140
Let’s stop there for a moment and think
about that.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:50.300
An AI that understands of an English description
mixed in with mathematical notation, and codes

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:57.200
up a solution as well as the average human
competitor, at least, on the tasks given in

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.260
this dataset.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.480
So, what is this wizardry, and what is the
key?

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.150
Well, let’s pop the hood, and have a look
together!

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.879
Oh yes!

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:16.349
One of the keys here is that it generates
a ton of candidate programs and is able to

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:20.069
filter them down to just a few promising solutions.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:24.120
And, it can do this quickly and accurately.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.409
This is huge.

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:27.599
Why?

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:33.610
Because this means that the AI is able to
have a quick look at a computer program and

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:39.260
tell with a pretty high accuracy whether this
will solve the given task or not.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.340
It has an intuition of sorts, if you will.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:51.390
Now, interestingly, it also uses 41 billion
parameters. 41 billion is tiny compared to

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:56.530
OpenAI’s GPT-3, which has 175 billion.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:03.569
This means that currently, AlphaCode uses
a more compact neural network, and it is possible

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:08.680
that the number of parameters can be increased
here to further improve the results.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:14.810
If we look at DeepMind’s track record of
improving on these ideas, I have no doubt

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:20.669
that however amazing these results seem now,
we have really seen nothing yet.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:26.789
And wait, there is more - this is where I
completely lost my papers: in the case that

00:04:26.789 --> 00:04:30.630
you see here, it even learned to invent algorithms.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:38.979
A simple one, mind you, this is DFS, a search
algorithm that is taught in first-year undergrad

00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:42.800
computer science courses, but that does not
matter.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.619
What matters is that this is an AI that can
finally, invent new things.

00:04:48.619 --> 00:04:49.619
Wow.

00:04:49.619 --> 00:04:56.370
A Google engineer, who is also a world-class
competitive programmer was also asked to have

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.069
a look at these solutions, and he was quite
happy with the results.

00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:07.159
He said to one of the solutions that “it
looks like it could easily be written by a

00:05:07.159 --> 00:05:08.159
human, very impressive!”

00:05:08.159 --> 00:05:14.529
Now, clearly, it is not perfect, and thus,
some criticisms were also voiced.

00:05:14.529 --> 00:05:20.430
For instance, sometimes it forgets about variables
that remain unused.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.610
Even that is very humanlike, I must say.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:29.349
But, do not think of this paper as the end
of something.

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:34.069
This is but a stepping stone towards something
much greater.

00:05:34.069 --> 00:05:40.020
And I will be honest - I can’t even imagine
what we will have just a couple more papers

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.499
down the line.

00:05:41.499 --> 00:05:43.210
What a time to be alive!

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.119
So, what would you use this for?

00:05:46.119 --> 00:05:49.219
What do you think will happen a couple papers
down the line?

00:05:49.219 --> 00:05:52.439
I’d love to know - let me know in the comments
below.

00:05:52.439 --> 00:05:58.059
And, if you are also excited to hear about
potential followup papers to this, make sure

00:05:58.059 --> 00:06:01.399
to subscribe and hit the bell icon.

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:05.979
You definitely not want to not miss it when
it comes!

00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:21.580
Thanks for watching and for 

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:56.039
your generous support, and I'll see you 
next time!

